course: biology
lesson: 229
title: Echolocation: how bats see with sound
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Shout «hello» in a big empty hall and a moment later the walls shout it back: that returning sound is an echo.
explain: A hunting bat does the same trick hundreds of times a second, with squeaks far too high for us to hear, and listens to the echoes.
explain: From the echo the bat's brain works out how far away a moth is, which way it is flying and even how big it is, so it «sees» with its ears.
explain: Watch bats round a streetlamp at dusk: they dart after gnats in the dark and never bump into each other, all thanks to echoes.
explain: Using echoes to find things is called echolocation, and bats had it for millions of years before people invented machines that do the same.
fact: Bats catch moths and gnats in the dark by shouting very high squeaks and listening for the echoes.
fact: Most bat calls are ultrasound, too high for grown-ups to hear; a bat detector turns them into clicks we can hear.
fact: No bat is blind: small bats see quite well in dim light, and big fruit bats called flying foxes find fruit by sight and smell and mostly do not echolocate.
fact: Just before it grabs a moth, a bat calls faster and faster, up to about 200 calls a second; this is called the feeding buzz.
fact: Some tiger moths click back at bats to jam their echoes or warn that they taste nasty, and some moths can hear bats and dive away; the fluffy scales on some moths' wings soak up sound, so the bat hears less echo.
fact: Bats rest upside down, gripping with their toes, so they can drop straight into flight, because taking off from the ground is hard for them; a lock in the toe tendons holds them without effort.
fact: Bracken Cave in Texas holds about 15 million bats in summer, and each mother finds her own pup by its call and smell; when they all fly out at dusk, the huge cloud of bats shows up on weather radar like a rain cloud.
fact: Real vampire bats are tiny, live in Central and South America and mostly lap blood from sleeping cows, horses and birds; they share food with hungry friends.
fact: Bats are the only mammals that can truly fly, and about one in five kinds of mammal is a bat.
fact: In the 1790s Lazzaro Spallanzani found that bats with covered eyes still dodged threads but bats with blocked ears crashed; only in 1938 did special microphones prove bats use ultrasound.
fact: A small bat can catch hundreds of midges in an hour, so bats are free pest control.
fact: Bat boxes give bats safe places to sleep as old hollow trees and barns disappear, and in Britain bats and their roosts are protected by law.
exercise: How does a bat find a moth in the dark? | It squeaks and listens for the echoes | It smells the moth from a whole mile away | It sees it through special night glasses | It feels the moth's heat with its wings
exercise: What does a bat detector do? | Turns bat squeaks too high for us into sounds we can hear | Makes bats come closer to the person holding it | Counts how many bats live in a park | Scares bats away from houses with a loud noise
exercise: Why can't grown-ups hear most bat calls? | The calls are ultrasound, too high for human ears | Bats call only when nobody is near | Bat calls are too quiet to hear | Bats call only under water, where our ears cannot follow
exercise: Is the saying «blind as a bat» true? | No, all bats can see, and fruit bats see very well | Yes, all bats are born blind | Yes, bats have no eyes at all, only ears | Only fruit bats are blind
exercise: How do flying foxes mostly find fruit? | By sight and smell | Only by their echoes | By touch with their tails | By following the moths
exercise: What is the feeding buzz? | A fast burst of calls just before a bat grabs a moth | The noise of a bat's wings | A song bats sing to each other after eating | The sound of a moth's wings
exercise: How do some tiger moths fight back against bats? | They click back to jam the echoes or warn that they taste nasty | They bite the bats' ears | They glow brightly so the bats are dazzled and fly off | They fly straight at the bat
exercise: Why do bats rest hanging upside down? | They can drop straight into flight, as taking off from the ground is hard | Their blood needs to go to their heads | They cannot stand on their feet at all | They are listening to the ground for insects walking about
exercise: Why does a sleeping bat not fall off its perch? | A lock in its toe tendons keeps the toes gripping without effort | It glues itself to the roof | It wakes up every minute or so to hold on tight with its claws | Other bats hold it up
exercise: How does a mother bat find her pup among millions in a cave? | By its call and its smell | By the colour of its fur coat | She never finds it again, ever | By counting from the entrance
exercise: What do real vampire bats mostly drink? | Blood from sleeping cows, horses and birds | People's blood from their necks | Fruit juice | Nectar from flowers
exercise: What do vampire bats do for a hungry friend? | Bring up some blood to share with it | Chase it out of the roost | Bite it | Teach it how to catch moths
exercise: What did Spallanzani find in the 1790s? | Bats with blocked ears crashed, but bats with covered eyes did not | Bats with covered eyes crashed | Bats can fly without wings | Bats use their noses to smell the threads and dodge them
exercise: Which animals are the only mammals that truly fly? | Bats | Flying squirrels | Flying fish | Sugar gliders
exercise: Why are bats good for a village? | They eat huge numbers of midges and other insects | They pollinate the potato flowers in every garden | They scare away foxes | They clean the moss off the roofs at night
exercise: Why are bat boxes put up in parks? | To give bats safe places to sleep as old hollow trees disappear | To catch bats so they can be sent to zoos | To keep birds out of the park | To feed bats with fruit left inside the boxes by the park keepers
test: «blind as a bat, my nan says, about my grandad lol» What is wrong with the saying? | No bat is blind; bats see, and many also use echoes | Nothing, it is right, because bats have no eyes at all | Bats are blind only in winter | Bats are blind, but grandads are not
test: A cartoon vampire turns into a big bat and bites people's necks. What is the real vampire bat like? | Tiny, and mostly drinks a little blood from sleeping animals | Huge, and drinks people's blood every night | It lives only in old castles in Transylvania and nowhere else | It eats garlic
test: What would happen if a hunting bat suddenly could not hear? | It would bump into things and miss insects in the dark | It would hunt better by smell | Nothing, because bats hunt only with their eyes, like owls do | It would fly higher and faster
test: «the detector's crackling like popcorn, then it goes brrrrr» What just happened? | A bat called faster and faster and probably caught an insect | The detector is broken | A moth sang a song | A bat fell asleep in the middle of the air and dropped to the ground
test: At a zoo dark house, children walk with eyes shut and bump into the walls, while the bats fly freely. Why? | Bats' brains turn echoes into a picture of the world | Bats have much bigger eyes than children do | The children's ears are blocked | Bats can see straight through walls with special eyes
test: «why'd it take 150 years to figure out the bat thing» What is the best answer? | Nobody could hear the sound until special microphones came in 1938 | Bats hid from scientists | Scientists did not care about bats | Bats only started using their echoes in 1938, so before that there was nothing to hear
test: Why can't a bat just jump off the ground and fly away like a pigeon? | Its wings are not strong enough for an easy take-off, so it drops from a perch | Bats are too heavy to fly | Bats never touch the ground in their lives | Bats can fly only at night, and the ground is always too cold to take off from
test: The flying bats from Bracken Cave show up on weather radar. What does the radar seem to see? | Something like a rain cloud | A big aeroplane flying very low | Snow falling softly from the sky | Nothing at all on the screen
test: «there's a bat in our loft, dad wants to smoke it out» What is the best advice? | Leave it and ask a bat group, as bats and roosts are protected in Britain | Smoke it out quickly | Catch it with your bare hands | Block the hole while it sleeps inside so that no more bats can come in
test: Count them: 6 bats hang in a row from a branch. Each bat holds on with both feet. How many feet hold the branch, and how many wings are folded? | 12 feet and 12 wings | 6 feet and 6 wings | 24 feet and 12 wings | 12 feet and 24 wings
test: Some moths have fluffy wing scales. How could that help them? | The fluff soaks up sound, so the bat hears less echo | The fluff makes them fly much faster | The fluff tickles the bat's nose so that it sneezes and lets go | The fluff makes them glow
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the method by which an insectivorous bat locates its prey in darkness? | It emits calls of high frequency and interprets the returning echoes | It relies solely upon the perception of light | It detects the warmth of its prey by means of sensitive organs situated in its wings | It is guided by the scent of nectar
exam: Identify the finding of Lazzaro Spallanzani in respect of bats flying amongst suspended threads. | Bats deprived of hearing collided with the threads, whereas those deprived of sight did not | Bats deprived of sight invariably collided with the threads | Bats were incapable of flight in any enclosed room | Bats avoided the threads by smell alone, irrespective of the condition of their eyes or ears
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vampire bat. | It is small and feeds chiefly upon the blood of sleeping animals such as cattle and birds | It is of great size and feeds principally upon human blood | It inhabits only the castles of Europe | It feeds exclusively upon fruit, which it locates by means of its keen sense of smell at night
exam: Ascertain the term by which the rapid succession of calls emitted by a bat immediately prior to the capture of an insect is designated. | The feeding buzz | The bee's waggle dance | The signature whistle | The birds' dawn chorus
